---
import FoundationPage from "../components/FoundationPage.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../components/CodeBlock.astro";

const run = `docker compose -f apps/maker/docker-compose.yml up -d   # PostgreSQL, once
pnpm --filter @skryensya/maker dev                        # http://localhost:4200`;

const outline = `page 3f2a… "Home" /
  9c1e… layout/Main
    41b7… layout/Stack gap="lg"
      a0d2… typography/Heading
        a0d3… "Build once, use it everywhere."
      77e9… button/Button.action
        77ea… "Get started"
      77f1… button/Button.action
        77f2… "Read the docs"`;

const apply = `{
  "project": "7d0c…",
  "revision": 4,
  "operations": [
    {
      "type": "page",
      "page": "3f2a…",
      "operations": [
        {
          "type": "wrap",
          "children": ["77e9…", "77f1…"],
          "with": { "contract": "layout", "signature": "Inline", "options": { "gap": "sm" } }
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}`;
---

<FoundationPage
  title="Maker"
  description="A visual page builder where the browser does the layout: pages are trees of signatures, changed only by operations, never by coordinates."
>
  <p class="lede">
    The Maker builds pages by composing the kit's own components inside ordinary document flow. There
    is no canvas and no coordinate: a page is a tree, <code>Stack</code>, <code>Inline</code>,
    <code>Grid</code>, <code>Box</code> and <code>Wrapper</code> say how its parts relate, and the
    browser works out where everything lands. What it exports is the same usage tree
    <code>validate_ui</code> accepts, emitted as React or HTML.
  </p>

  <h2>Run it</h2>
  <p>
    The Maker is a local app in <code>apps/maker</code>. Its projects live in PostgreSQL, which the
    compose file starts on port 5433 (<code>DATABASE_URL</code> points elsewhere). Several projects can be
    open at once, each in a tab with its own history; open tabs come back on the next visit. Without a
    database the Maker still works, keeping one site in the browser, and says so.
  </p>
  <p>
    A new project can start as any page of the <a href="/templates">templates gallery</a>, from the
    projects panel or from the gallery's own <em>Open in Maker</em> link.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={run} label="Shell" lang="bash" />

  <h2>The one rule</h2>
  <p>
    Nothing is ever placed by position. There is no <code>x</code>, no <code>top</code>, no
    <code>absolute</code>, no pixel width, no negative margin and no transform used for layout, and the
    reason is not a lint rule: <strong>no operation can express one</strong>. Every change is one of a
    closed set, and a <em>place</em> in that set is a parent, a slot and an index among siblings. You
    decide that two buttons belong in a row; the browser decides where the row breaks.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Things one above another:</strong> a <code>Stack</code>, spaced by its <code>gap</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Things side by side:</strong> an <code>Inline</code>. With <code>wrap</code> on, the browser moves them to a new line when the room runs out.</li>
    <li><strong>A real grid:</strong> a <code>Grid</code>. With <code>minColumn</code>, its own width decides how many columns fit, with no breakpoint.</li>
    <li><strong>A page column:</strong> a <code>Wrapper</code>, centred, on the size scale.</li>
    <li><strong>A region with its own surface or a ceiling on its width:</strong> a <code>Box</code>, with <code>padding</code>, <code>surface</code>, <code>border</code> or <code>measure</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>A child that takes what a row leaves:</strong> <code>sizing: fill</code>, which the Inline offers to its children and a child loses when it moves elsewhere.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The inspector shows the contract, and only the contract</h2>
  <p>
    Selecting a node shows what it is, which parent holds it, its position among its siblings and its
    <em>layout role</em>: what it is to its parent, in the parent's terms ("item in a row that wraps;
    fills the leftover space"). Below that come the options its signature declares, and nothing else.
    A Stack has no padding field because a Stack has no padding: padding is a Box's, and "Wrap in Box"
    is one click away. When two options decide the same thing, such as a Grid's <code>columns</code>
    and <code>minColumn</code>, the inspector asks which one decides, and switching clears the other.
  </p>
  <p>
    A page may be <strong>pending</strong>: an empty container, a missing accessible name, a link to a
    path no page has. Pending is shown on the node and reported, never repaired for you, and it never
    blocks an export. What is never allowed is broken <em>structure</em>: a drop zone only appears
    where the contract accepts the node, so a Wrapper cannot land inside a Wrapper, and a block cannot
    land inside a heading or a button.
  </p>

  <h2>The bar</h2>
  <p>
    Every command is a word in the <a href="/components/app-bar">AppBar</a> across the top: File (new,
    close, export, publish), Edit (undo, redo, select the parent, and the actions on the selection),
    View (edit or interact, the stage width, the preview, zoom, the panels), Page, Insert and Help.
    The width and the zoom are also on its right, next to whether the project is saved. The open
    projects are tabs under it. Clicking a blank part of a page selects the page itself (its Main), so
    after a section fills the page, the next insert goes below it.
  </p>

  <h2>The stage is a real browser</h2>
  <p>
    The page renders in its own document with the real React binding, as wide as the width you pick
    and not a pixel different, so media queries and container widths answer exactly as they will in
    production. Changing the width changes the room the browser has and nothing in the page. In
    <em>Edit</em> mode a click selects; in <em>Interact</em> mode the page responds, and a link to
    another page of the site opens it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Every page of the site sits side by side on a canvas you pan and zoom, like a design tool; click a
    page to open it. The canvas is only a view: zooming scales the picture, never the width the page
    is laid out at, and where you left the camera is not part of the site.
  </p>

  <h2>Pages</h2>
  <p>
    A site is a list of pages, each with a name, a path such as <code>/about</code> and its own tree.
    Adding, renaming, moving and removing a page are operations too, in the same history, so removing
    a page is undone like removing a button. Links between pages are ordinary <code>href</code>
    options; the inspector suggests the site's paths, and a link to a path no page has is pending.
  </p>

  <h2 id="keyboard">Keyboard</h2>
  <p>With the layers panel focused, every structural action has a shortcut, and the toolbar at the top of the inspector and the bar's Edit menu run exactly the same actions.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><kbd class="sk-kbd">Esc</kbd>: select the container of what is selected, up to the page's Main, then nothing. With Main selected, whatever you insert goes to the end of the page.</li>
    <li><kbd class="sk-kbd">Alt</kbd> + <kbd class="sk-kbd">↑</kbd> / <kbd class="sk-kbd">↓</kbd>: move before the previous or after the next sibling.</li>
    <li><kbd class="sk-kbd">Alt</kbd> + <kbd class="sk-kbd">←</kbd>: move out of the container; <kbd class="sk-kbd">Alt</kbd> + <kbd class="sk-kbd">→</kbd>: into the previous one.</li>
    <li><kbd class="sk-kbd">⌘G</kbd> wraps in a Stack, <kbd class="sk-kbd">⌘⇧G</kbd> unwraps, <kbd class="sk-kbd">⌘D</kbd> duplicates, <kbd class="sk-kbd">Delete</kbd> removes.</li>
    <li><kbd class="sk-kbd">⌘Z</kbd> and <kbd class="sk-kbd">⌘⇧Z</kbd> undo and redo, anywhere outside a text field.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Editing with an agent</h2>
  <p>
    A prompt follows the same rule. The MCP server run from the checkout offers
    <code>maker_projects</code>, <code>maker_read</code>, which returns a project as an outline with an
    id on every node, and <code>maker_apply</code>, which takes operations and nothing else. Ask an agent to "put these two
    buttons next to each other" and the only thing it can send is a wrap in an Inline.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={outline} label="maker_read" lang="yaml" />
  <CodeBlock code={apply} label="maker_apply" lang="json" />
  <p>
    The change is saved to the project, any Maker that has it open shows it at once, and it is one step
    in the history: undo takes it back. Passing the <code>revision</code> it read means an agent never
    overwrites something you did in the meantime; the request is refused with the current outline
    instead. An operation the contract refuses (an option the signature does not declare, a Wrapper
    inside a Wrapper) refuses the whole request, with the reason.
  </p>

  <h2>Publish</h2>
  <p>
    A project is published as a static site at <code>https://&lt;name&gt;.skryensya.dev/</code>: one page
    per path, with the kit's stylesheets and enhancers, served by one Cloudflare Worker. Only the
    machine that holds the publish token can publish. A link that would run code
    (<code>javascript:</code>, a <code>data:</code> document) keeps the whole site from being published,
    and every page is served with a policy that runs the kit and no other script. The one-time setup is
    in <code>apps/sites-worker/README.md</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Export</h2>
  <p>
    The open page exports as React, HTML with the stylesheets it needs, or the usage tree; the whole
    site exports as one component per page, or as a site file, which the Maker opens again as a new
    project. Every form
    comes from the same emitter <code>validate_ui</code> uses.
  </p>
</FoundationPage>
